    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Moderate asthma exacerbation triggered by a viral cold (J45.41)</content>. Peak flow 420 L/min, 69 percent of personal best, with oxygen saturation 95 percent and no features of a severe or life-threatening attack. Two days of increased reliever use have not brought him back to the green zone, so a short oral corticosteroid course is indicated. No signs of pneumonia, so a chest radiograph and antibiotics are not indicated. [S]<list><item>Prednisone 40 mg each morning for 5 days, with food; no taper needed for a short course.</item><item>Continue budesonide-formoterol two inhalations twice daily, and one inhalation as needed, up to 12 in total a day.</item><item>Inhaler technique checked with his own device and spacer: correct.</item><item>Rest from boxing and hard rowing until peak flow is back above 488 L/min.</item><item>Action plan updated: return or go to the emergency department for breathlessness at rest, peak flow below 305 L/min (50 percent), needing more than 12 inhalations a day, or poor relief.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Moderate persistent asthma (J45.40)</content>. Well controlled before this cold. This is his first exacerbation since starting controller therapy, and it raises his future exacerbation risk. [S]<list><item>Clinic review within one week of this exacerbation, by the CMID schedule.</item><item>Influenza vaccine as soon as this season&apos;s vaccine arrives.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); 2020 Focused Updates to the Asthma Management Guidelines: A Report from the National Asthma Education and Prevention Program Coordinating Committee Expert Panel Working Group (2020); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, asthma monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in three days. Clinic review within one week. Return the same day or go to the emergency department for red-zone symptoms. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-14: six-month asthma review after a summer exacerbation. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
